{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queuing stores operations in an ordered buffer and processes them individually. It is the primary Pacer strategy for work that should not be discarded when calls arrive faster than they can run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queues are lossless only while they have capacity. A finite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":", explicit clearing, expiration, or a processing error can still remove or reject work."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-queuing-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How queuing works"}]},{"type":"code","value":"Queuing (process one item every 2 ticks)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️     ⬇️  ⬇️     ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️\nQueue:       [ABC]   [BC]    [BCDE]    [DE]    [E]    []\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅       ✅        ✅      ✅     ✅\n             [======================================================]\n             ^ Accepted items remain queued until processed\n\n             [Items arrive]    [Process steadily]      [Empty]","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The queue can process automatically with a delay between items, or remain stopped for manual processing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-use-queuing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to use queuing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose queuing when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every accepted operation should run individually."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Processing order matters."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Incoming work may temporarily exceed processing capacity."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A maximum buffer size should reject excess work explicitly."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"FIFO, LIFO, or priority ordering is required."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose another utility when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only the final call matters. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./debouncing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"debouncing"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intermediate calls may be discarded while work runs steadily. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./throttling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"throttling"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls should be rejected after a time-window quota. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./rate-limiting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rate limiting"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items should run together. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./batching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"batching"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tasks return Promises or should run concurrently. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"async queuing"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-queuing-in-tanstack-pacer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using queuing in TanStack Pacer"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Pacer provides two core APIs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queue"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a function that adds an item to an automatically running queue."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Queuer"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes ordering, lifecycle, capacity, expiration, callbacks, and state."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"convenience-function","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Convenience function"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { queue } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst processItem = queue<number>(\n  (item) => {\n    console.log('Processing:', item)\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nprocessItem(1) // true, accepted and processed immediately.\nprocessItem(2) // true, accepted and queued.\nprocessItem(3) // true, accepted and queued.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The returned function reports whether the item was accepted. It does not expose queue lifecycle methods."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"class-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Class API"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Queuer } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst queuer = new Queuer<number>(\n  (item) => {\n    console.log('Processing:', item)\n  },\n  {\n    wait: 1000,\n    maxSize: 10,\n  },\n)\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\n\nconsole.log(queuer.peekAllItems())","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialItems"},{"type":"text","value":" when work is already available at creation time. The queue applies its normal insertion and capacity rules, and automatic processing can begin immediately unless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started: false"},{"type":"text","value":" is set."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"results-and-errors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Results and errors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The synchronous queuer does not retain the wrapped function's return value or catch errors. It removes an item before invoking the function. If the function throws, that item is no longer queued and the error propagates from the current execution."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"async queuing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for Promise results, configurable error handling, retries, abort support, and concurrency."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ordering-items","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordering items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic processing uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItemsTo"},{"type":"text","value":" to choose where new items enter and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getItemsFrom"},{"type":"text","value":" to choose where items leave."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"fifo","children":[{"type":"text","value":"FIFO"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"FIFO processes the oldest item first. This is the default."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  addItemsTo: 'back',\n  getItemsFrom: 'front',\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.addItem(3)\nqueuer.start() // Processes 1, 2, 3.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"lifo","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LIFO"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LIFO processes the newest item first."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  addItemsTo: 'back',\n  getItemsFrom: 'back',\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.addItem(3)\nqueuer.start() // Processes 3, 2, 1.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"priority","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Priority"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPriority"},{"type":"text","value":" to process higher numeric priorities first. Priority ordering takes precedence over front and back retrieval."}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Task = { name: string; priority: number }\n\nconst queuer = new Queuer<Task>(processTask, {\n  getPriority: (task) => task.priority,\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'low', priority: 1 })\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'high', priority: 3 })\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'medium', priority: 2 })\nqueuer.start() // Processes high, medium, low.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-and-manual-processing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic and manual processing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queues start automatically by default. The first accepted item processes immediately, then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" controls the delay before later items."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  wait: 1000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to collect items before processing:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, { started: false })\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.start()\nqueuer.stop()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" cancels the scheduled tick and retains queued items. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start()"},{"type":"text","value":" resumes automatic processing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For manual control:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes and processes the next item immediately."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getNextItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes and returns the next item without processing it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"peekNextItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the next item without removing it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"peekAllItems()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a copy of the current queue."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"capacity-and-rejection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capacity and rejection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" to bound the number of waiting items. An item added to a full queue is rejected, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject"},{"type":"text","value":" runs."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  maxSize: 2,\n  started: false,\n  onReject: (item, queuer) => {\n    console.log('Rejected:', item)\n    console.log('Total rejections:', queuer.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1) // true\nqueuer.addItem(2) // true\nqueuer.addItem(3) // false","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The active synchronous execution is not part of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":" counts items still waiting in the queue."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"expiring-stale-items","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expiring stale items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expirationDuration"},{"type":"text","value":" to remove items that have waited too long:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  expirationDuration: 5000,\n  onExpire: (item) => {\n    console.log('Expired:', item)\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsExpired"},{"type":"text","value":" for custom logic:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  getIsExpired: (item, addedAt) => Date.now() - addedAt > item.maxAge,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expiration is checked while the automatic processing loop runs. A stopped queue evaluates stale items when processing resumes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"flushing-clearing-and-resetting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flushing, clearing, and resetting"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"flush","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flush"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" processes waiting items immediately without the configured delay. Pass a count to process only part of the queue."}]},{"type":"code","value":"queuer.flush() // Process all waiting items.\nqueuer.flush(2) // Process at most two waiting items.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flushAsBatch()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes all waiting items and passes them to a separate batch function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"queuer.flushAsBatch((items) => {\n  saveItems(items)\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"clear","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clear"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes all waiting items without processing them. It does not change whether the queue is running."}]},{"type":"code","value":"queuer.clear()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reset","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reset"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" restores state to the default running, empty queue. It does not clear an already scheduled timeout. Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" when scheduled work must be canceled."}]},{"type":"code","value":"queuer.stop()\nqueuer.reset()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuring-and-observing-the-queue","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuring and observing the queue"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" to update future behavior. Changing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started"},{"type":"text","value":" through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" does not call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"queuer.setOptions({ wait: 250, maxSize: 20 })\nqueuer.start()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" option may be a function that receives the queuer instance:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  wait: (queuer) => (queuer.store.state.size > 20 ? 50 : 250),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use callbacks for queue events:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onItemsChange"},{"type":"text","value":": An item was added or removed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onExecute"},{"type":"text","value":": An item was processed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject"},{"type":"text","value":": An item was rejected."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onExpire"},{"type":"text","value":": An item expired."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To share a type-checked configuration across instances, define it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queuerOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" can restore selected queue state that your app has persisted. If it includes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":", they take precedence over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialItems"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.isRunning"},{"type":"text","value":" likewise takes precedence over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started"},{"type":"text","value":". Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Commonly useful state includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":": Items still waiting."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isRunning"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether automatic processing is enabled."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isIdle"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether a running queue is empty."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFull"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" has been reached."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Items whose wrapped function returned successfully."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rejectionCount"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expirationCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Items removed without processing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'idle'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'running'"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'stopped'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../reference/classes/Queuer.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Queuer"},{"type":"text","value":" API reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for complete option and state types."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-queuing-works","text":"How queuing works","level":2},{"id":"when-to-use-queuing","text":"When to use queuing","level":2},{"id":"using-queuing-in-tanstack-pacer","text":"Using queuing in TanStack Pacer","level":2},{"id":"convenience-function","text":"Convenience function","level":3},{"id":"class-api","text":"Class API","level":3},{"id":"results-and-errors","text":"Results and errors","level":3},{"id":"ordering-items","text":"Ordering items","level":2},{"id":"fifo","text":"FIFO","level":3},{"id":"lifo","text":"LIFO","level":3},{"id":"priority","text":"Priority","level":3},{"id":"automatic-and-manual-processing","text":"Automatic and manual processing","level":2},{"id":"capacity-and-rejection","text":"Capacity and rejection","level":2},{"id":"expiring-stale-items","text":"Expiring stale items","level":2},{"id":"flushing-clearing-and-resetting","text":"Flushing, clearing, and resetting","level":2},{"id":"flush","text":"Flush","level":3},{"id":"clear","text":"Clear","level":3},{"id":"reset","text":"Reset","level":3},{"id":"configuring-and-observing-the-queue","text":"Configuring and observing the queue","level":2},{"id":"state","text":"State","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nQueuing stores operations in an ordered buffer and processes them individually. It is the primary Pacer strategy for work that should not be discarded when calls arrive faster than they can run.\n\nQueues are lossless only while they have capacity. A finite `maxSize`, explicit clearing, expiration, or a processing error can still remove or reject work.\n\n## How queuing works\n\n```text\nQueuing (process one item every 2 ticks)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️     ⬇️  ⬇️     ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️\nQueue:       [ABC]   [BC]    [BCDE]    [DE]    [E]    []\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅       ✅        ✅      ✅     ✅\n             [======================================================]\n             ^ Accepted items remain queued until processed\n\n             [Items arrive]    [Process steadily]      [Empty]\n```\n\nThe queue can process automatically with a delay between items, or remain stopped for manual processing.\n\n## When to use queuing\n\nChoose queuing when:\n\n- Every accepted operation should run individually.\n- Processing order matters.\n- Incoming work may temporarily exceed processing capacity.\n- A maximum buffer size should reject excess work explicitly.\n- FIFO, LIFO, or priority ordering is required.\n\nChoose another utility when:\n\n- Only the final call matters. Use [debouncing](./debouncing.md).\n- Intermediate calls may be discarded while work runs steadily. Use [throttling](./throttling.md).\n- Calls should be rejected after a time-window quota. Use [rate limiting](./rate-limiting.md).\n- Items should run together. Use [batching](./batching.md).\n- Tasks return Promises or should run concurrently. Use [async queuing](./async-queuing.md).\n\n## Using queuing in TanStack Pacer\n\nTanStack Pacer provides two core APIs:\n\n- `queue` returns a function that adds an item to an automatically running queue.\n- `Queuer` exposes ordering, lifecycle, capacity, expiration, callbacks, and state.\n\n### Convenience function\n\n```ts\nimport { queue } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst processItem = queue<number>(\n  (item) => {\n    console.log('Processing:', item)\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nprocessItem(1) // true, accepted and processed immediately.\nprocessItem(2) // true, accepted and queued.\nprocessItem(3) // true, accepted and queued.\n```\n\nThe returned function reports whether the item was accepted. It does not expose queue lifecycle methods.\n\n### Class API\n\n```ts\nimport { Queuer } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst queuer = new Queuer<number>(\n  (item) => {\n    console.log('Processing:', item)\n  },\n  {\n    wait: 1000,\n    maxSize: 10,\n  },\n)\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\n\nconsole.log(queuer.peekAllItems())\n```\n\nPass `initialItems` when work is already available at creation time. The queue applies its normal insertion and capacity rules, and automatic processing can begin immediately unless `started: false` is set.\n\n### Results and errors\n\nThe synchronous queuer does not retain the wrapped function's return value or catch errors. It removes an item before invoking the function. If the function throws, that item is no longer queued and the error propagates from the current execution.\n\nUse [async queuing](./async-queuing.md) for Promise results, configurable error handling, retries, abort support, and concurrency.\n\n## Ordering items\n\nAutomatic processing uses `addItemsTo` to choose where new items enter and `getItemsFrom` to choose where items leave.\n\n### FIFO\n\nFIFO processes the oldest item first. This is the default.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  addItemsTo: 'back',\n  getItemsFrom: 'front',\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.addItem(3)\nqueuer.start() // Processes 1, 2, 3.\n```\n\n### LIFO\n\nLIFO processes the newest item first.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  addItemsTo: 'back',\n  getItemsFrom: 'back',\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.addItem(3)\nqueuer.start() // Processes 3, 2, 1.\n```\n\n### Priority\n\nProvide `getPriority` to process higher numeric priorities first. Priority ordering takes precedence over front and back retrieval.\n\n```ts\ntype Task = { name: string; priority: number }\n\nconst queuer = new Queuer<Task>(processTask, {\n  getPriority: (task) => task.priority,\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'low', priority: 1 })\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'high', priority: 3 })\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'medium', priority: 2 })\nqueuer.start() // Processes high, medium, low.\n```\n\n## Automatic and manual processing\n\nQueues start automatically by default. The first accepted item processes immediately, then `wait` controls the delay before later items.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  wait: 1000,\n})\n```\n\nSet `started: false` to collect items before processing:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, { started: false })\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.start()\nqueuer.stop()\n```\n\n`stop()` cancels the scheduled tick and retains queued items. `start()` resumes automatic processing.\n\nFor manual control:\n\n- `execute()` removes and processes the next item immediately.\n- `getNextItem()` removes and returns the next item without processing it.\n- `peekNextItem()` returns the next item without removing it.\n- `peekAllItems()` returns a copy of the current queue.\n\n## Capacity and rejection\n\nSet `maxSize` to bound the number of waiting items. An item added to a full queue is rejected, `addItem()` returns `false`, and `onReject` runs.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  maxSize: 2,\n  started: false,\n  onReject: (item, queuer) => {\n    console.log('Rejected:', item)\n    console.log('Total rejections:', queuer.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1) // true\nqueuer.addItem(2) // true\nqueuer.addItem(3) // false\n```\n\nThe active synchronous execution is not part of `size`; `size` counts items still waiting in the queue.\n\n## Expiring stale items\n\nUse `expirationDuration` to remove items that have waited too long:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  expirationDuration: 5000,\n  onExpire: (item) => {\n    console.log('Expired:', item)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse `getIsExpired` for custom logic:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  getIsExpired: (item, addedAt) => Date.now() - addedAt > item.maxAge,\n})\n```\n\nExpiration is checked while the automatic processing loop runs. A stopped queue evaluates stale items when processing resumes.\n\n## Flushing, clearing, and resetting\n\n### Flush\n\n`flush()` processes waiting items immediately without the configured delay. Pass a count to process only part of the queue.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.flush() // Process all waiting items.\nqueuer.flush(2) // Process at most two waiting items.\n```\n\n`flushAsBatch()` removes all waiting items and passes them to a separate batch function:\n\n```ts\nqueuer.flushAsBatch((items) => {\n  saveItems(items)\n})\n```\n\n### Clear\n\n`clear()` removes all waiting items without processing them. It does not change whether the queue is running.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.clear()\n```\n\n### Reset\n\n`reset()` restores state to the default running, empty queue. It does not clear an already scheduled timeout. Call `stop()` before `reset()` when scheduled work must be canceled.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.stop()\nqueuer.reset()\n```\n\n## Configuring and observing the queue\n\nUse `setOptions()` to update future behavior. Changing `started` through `setOptions()` does not call `start()` or `stop()`.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.setOptions({ wait: 250, maxSize: 20 })\nqueuer.start()\n```\n\nThe `wait` option may be a function that receives the queuer instance:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  wait: (queuer) => (queuer.store.state.size > 20 ? 50 : 250),\n})\n```\n\nUse callbacks for queue events:\n\n- `onItemsChange`: An item was added or removed.\n- `onExecute`: An item was processed.\n- `onReject`: An item was rejected.\n- `onExpire`: An item expired.\n\nTo share a type-checked configuration across instances, define it with `queuerOptions()`.\n\n## State\n\n`initialState` can restore selected queue state that your app has persisted. If it includes `items`, they take precedence over `initialItems`; `initialState.isRunning` likewise takes precedence over `started`. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful state includes:\n\n- `items` and `size`: Items still waiting.\n- `isRunning`: Whether automatic processing is enabled.\n- `isIdle`: Whether a running queue is empty.\n- `isFull`: Whether `maxSize` has been reached.\n- `executionCount`: Items whose wrapped function returned successfully.\n- `rejectionCount` and `expirationCount`: Items removed without processing.\n- `status`: `'idle'`, `'running'`, or `'stopped'`.\n\nSee the [`Queuer` API reference](../../../reference/classes/Queuer.md) for complete option and state types.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Vanilla Queuing Guide\nid: queuing\n---\n\nQueuing stores operations in an ordered buffer and processes them individually. It is the primary Pacer strategy for work that should not be discarded when calls arrive faster than they can run.\n\nQueues are lossless only while they have capacity. A finite `maxSize`, explicit clearing, expiration, or a processing error can still remove or reject work.\n\n## How queuing works\n\n```text\nQueuing (process one item every 2 ticks)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️     ⬇️  ⬇️     ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️\nQueue:       [ABC]   [BC]    [BCDE]    [DE]    [E]    []\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅       ✅        ✅      ✅     ✅\n             [======================================================]\n             ^ Accepted items remain queued until processed\n\n             [Items arrive]    [Process steadily]      [Empty]\n```\n\nThe queue can process automatically with a delay between items, or remain stopped for manual processing.\n\n## When to use queuing\n\nChoose queuing when:\n\n- Every accepted operation should run individually.\n- Processing order matters.\n- Incoming work may temporarily exceed processing capacity.\n- A maximum buffer size should reject excess work explicitly.\n- FIFO, LIFO, or priority ordering is required.\n\nChoose another utility when:\n\n- Only the final call matters. Use [debouncing](./debouncing.md).\n- Intermediate calls may be discarded while work runs steadily. Use [throttling](./throttling.md).\n- Calls should be rejected after a time-window quota. Use [rate limiting](./rate-limiting.md).\n- Items should run together. Use [batching](./batching.md).\n- Tasks return Promises or should run concurrently. Use [async queuing](./async-queuing.md).\n\n## Using queuing in TanStack Pacer\n\nTanStack Pacer provides two core APIs:\n\n- `queue` returns a function that adds an item to an automatically running queue.\n- `Queuer` exposes ordering, lifecycle, capacity, expiration, callbacks, and state.\n\n### Convenience function\n\n```ts\nimport { queue } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst processItem = queue<number>(\n  (item) => {\n    console.log('Processing:', item)\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nprocessItem(1) // true, accepted and processed immediately.\nprocessItem(2) // true, accepted and queued.\nprocessItem(3) // true, accepted and queued.\n```\n\nThe returned function reports whether the item was accepted. It does not expose queue lifecycle methods.\n\n### Class API\n\n```ts\nimport { Queuer } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst queuer = new Queuer<number>(\n  (item) => {\n    console.log('Processing:', item)\n  },\n  {\n    wait: 1000,\n    maxSize: 10,\n  },\n)\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\n\nconsole.log(queuer.peekAllItems())\n```\n\nPass `initialItems` when work is already available at creation time. The queue applies its normal insertion and capacity rules, and automatic processing can begin immediately unless `started: false` is set.\n\n### Results and errors\n\nThe synchronous queuer does not retain the wrapped function's return value or catch errors. It removes an item before invoking the function. If the function throws, that item is no longer queued and the error propagates from the current execution.\n\nUse [async queuing](./async-queuing.md) for Promise results, configurable error handling, retries, abort support, and concurrency.\n\n## Ordering items\n\nAutomatic processing uses `addItemsTo` to choose where new items enter and `getItemsFrom` to choose where items leave.\n\n### FIFO\n\nFIFO processes the oldest item first. This is the default.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  addItemsTo: 'back',\n  getItemsFrom: 'front',\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.addItem(3)\nqueuer.start() // Processes 1, 2, 3.\n```\n\n### LIFO\n\nLIFO processes the newest item first.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  addItemsTo: 'back',\n  getItemsFrom: 'back',\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.addItem(3)\nqueuer.start() // Processes 3, 2, 1.\n```\n\n### Priority\n\nProvide `getPriority` to process higher numeric priorities first. Priority ordering takes precedence over front and back retrieval.\n\n```ts\ntype Task = { name: string; priority: number }\n\nconst queuer = new Queuer<Task>(processTask, {\n  getPriority: (task) => task.priority,\n  started: false,\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'low', priority: 1 })\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'high', priority: 3 })\nqueuer.addItem({ name: 'medium', priority: 2 })\nqueuer.start() // Processes high, medium, low.\n```\n\n## Automatic and manual processing\n\nQueues start automatically by default. The first accepted item processes immediately, then `wait` controls the delay before later items.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  wait: 1000,\n})\n```\n\nSet `started: false` to collect items before processing:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, { started: false })\n\nqueuer.addItem(1)\nqueuer.addItem(2)\nqueuer.start()\nqueuer.stop()\n```\n\n`stop()` cancels the scheduled tick and retains queued items. `start()` resumes automatic processing.\n\nFor manual control:\n\n- `execute()` removes and processes the next item immediately.\n- `getNextItem()` removes and returns the next item without processing it.\n- `peekNextItem()` returns the next item without removing it.\n- `peekAllItems()` returns a copy of the current queue.\n\n## Capacity and rejection\n\nSet `maxSize` to bound the number of waiting items. An item added to a full queue is rejected, `addItem()` returns `false`, and `onReject` runs.\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  maxSize: 2,\n  started: false,\n  onReject: (item, queuer) => {\n    console.log('Rejected:', item)\n    console.log('Total rejections:', queuer.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})\n\nqueuer.addItem(1) // true\nqueuer.addItem(2) // true\nqueuer.addItem(3) // false\n```\n\nThe active synchronous execution is not part of `size`; `size` counts items still waiting in the queue.\n\n## Expiring stale items\n\nUse `expirationDuration` to remove items that have waited too long:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  expirationDuration: 5000,\n  onExpire: (item) => {\n    console.log('Expired:', item)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse `getIsExpired` for custom logic:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  getIsExpired: (item, addedAt) => Date.now() - addedAt > item.maxAge,\n})\n```\n\nExpiration is checked while the automatic processing loop runs. A stopped queue evaluates stale items when processing resumes.\n\n## Flushing, clearing, and resetting\n\n### Flush\n\n`flush()` processes waiting items immediately without the configured delay. Pass a count to process only part of the queue.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.flush() // Process all waiting items.\nqueuer.flush(2) // Process at most two waiting items.\n```\n\n`flushAsBatch()` removes all waiting items and passes them to a separate batch function:\n\n```ts\nqueuer.flushAsBatch((items) => {\n  saveItems(items)\n})\n```\n\n### Clear\n\n`clear()` removes all waiting items without processing them. It does not change whether the queue is running.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.clear()\n```\n\n### Reset\n\n`reset()` restores state to the default running, empty queue. It does not clear an already scheduled timeout. Call `stop()` before `reset()` when scheduled work must be canceled.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.stop()\nqueuer.reset()\n```\n\n## Configuring and observing the queue\n\nUse `setOptions()` to update future behavior. Changing `started` through `setOptions()` does not call `start()` or `stop()`.\n\n```ts\nqueuer.setOptions({ wait: 250, maxSize: 20 })\nqueuer.start()\n```\n\nThe `wait` option may be a function that receives the queuer instance:\n\n```ts\nconst queuer = new Queuer(processItem, {\n  wait: (queuer) => (queuer.store.state.size > 20 ? 50 : 250),\n})\n```\n\nUse callbacks for queue events:\n\n- `onItemsChange`: An item was added or removed.\n- `onExecute`: An item was processed.\n- `onReject`: An item was rejected.\n- `onExpire`: An item expired.\n\nTo share a type-checked configuration across instances, define it with `queuerOptions()`.\n\n## State\n\n`initialState` can restore selected queue state that your app has persisted. If it includes `items`, they take precedence over `initialItems`; `initialState.isRunning` likewise takes precedence over `started`. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful state includes:\n\n- `items` and `size`: Items still waiting.\n- `isRunning`: Whether automatic processing is enabled.\n- `isIdle`: Whether a running queue is empty.\n- `isFull`: Whether `maxSize` has been reached.\n- `executionCount`: Items whose wrapped function returned successfully.\n- `rejectionCount` and `expirationCount`: Items removed without processing.\n- `status`: `'idle'`, `'running'`, or `'stopped'`.\n\nSee the [`Queuer` API reference](../../../reference/classes/Queuer.md) for complete option and state types.\n"}
